/// One entry of a reverse (unicode -> byte) table.
struct Reverse
{
    char16_t unicode;
    unsigned char byte;
};

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The tables below are generated from the vendor mappings published by
// unicode.org and were checked byte for byte against ICU's own converters.
// Do not edit by hand.
//
// Only the upper half is tabulated; bytes below 0x80 are ASCII, as both the
// vendor mappings and QTextCodec had them.
//
// The bytes a mapping leaves undefined (0x98 in windows-1251; 0x81, 0x8D, 0x8F,
// 0x90 and 0x9D in windows-1252) are mapped to the C1 control point of the same
// value, which is what ICU does as well. That keeps every table a bijection, so
// a file that is not really in this encoding still survives a load and a save
// instead of losing those bytes.
//
// One deliberate difference from ICU: its IBM866 (like all its IBM-* tables)
// applies the IBM control-code swap, decoding 0x1A to U+001C, 0x1C to U+007F
// and 0x7F to U+001A. The unicode.org CP866.TXT mapping, the WHATWG ibm866
// index and Qt 5's QTextCodec all leave the ASCII range alone, and so do we:
// a file saved by an older build has to read back unchanged, and a stray 0x1A
// (Ctrl-Z, the DOS end-of-file mark) in a script must not come back as 0x7F.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

